config SOC_FAMILY_LS2K300
	select LOONGARCH
	select GEN_SW_ISR_TABLE

if SOC_FAMILY_LS2K300

rsource "*/Kconfig"

config LOONGARCH_UBOOT_UIMAGE
	bool "Generate a u-boot legacy uImage (zephyr.uImage)"
	select BUILD_OUTPUT_BIN
	default y
	help
	  Emit an additional zephyr.uImage in the u-boot legacy image format so
	  that the 2K0300 boards can be booted with the same flow as the vendor
	  kernel, e.g.

	    tftpboot ${loadaddr} zephyr.uImage
	    bootm ${loadaddr}

	  u-boot calls the LoongArch architecture "loongarch" in its image
	  architecture table, so a plain legacy uImage works for LoongArch too.
	  The header is produced by soc/loongson/ls2k0300/gen_uimage.py, which
	  takes the load address and the entry point from zephyr.elf; u-boot
	  relocates the payload to the load address stored in the header, so
	  the file can be transferred to any free address first.

config LS2K0300_RX_CHECK
	bool "Console receive interrupt self check"
	help
	  Run one non blocking check three seconds after the application starts,
	  from a work queue item so it cannot starve anything:

	    1. print IER / IIR / LSR plus the EIOINTC enable, routing, device
	       status and routed status, and ESTAT,
	    2. put the console UART into internal loopback (MCR bit 4) and write
	       one byte to it,

	  so the byte comes back as received data. If the receive interrupt path
	  works the driver ISR takes it immediately and LSR.DR reads 0 and ESTAT
	  has long settled; if the byte is still sitting in the FIFO with ESTAT
	  clear, then the UART is not asking for an interrupt at all, and if
	  ESTAT is set while the byte stays, the dispatch is the break.

	  Needed because every static check said the path was enabled - IER bit 0
	  set, EIOINTC vector 0 enabled, ECFG bit 3 set, paging off - while the
	  shell never received a character.

config LS2K0300_CONSOLE_GUARD
	bool "Watch the console UART and repair it"
	default y
	help
	  Check the console UART once a second from the system work queue and
	  repair the states that make the shell look dead while the rest of the
	  system keeps running:

	    - the receive pin no longer on function 3, i.e. moved out of uart0_rx
	      by something writing the pin mux field (the SoC itself comes up
	      with the pin in its GPIO setting). The console keeps printing
	      because the transmitter's field is still right, and nothing at all
	      arrives. The field is written back.
	    - the UART left in internal loopback (MCR.LOOP set, and OUT2 clear
	      with it): the receiver is off the RX pin, so the keyboard never
	      arrives, while the shell's own output is fed straight back into the
	      receiver. MCR is written back to DTR | RTS | OUT2.
	    - a received byte that nobody serves for two ticks: a receive
	      interrupt that was lost on the way to the driver. IER is toggled so
	      the UART raises the request again.
	    - shell output queued (IER.TBE set) while the transmitter sits idle
	      (THRE set) for two ticks: the empty-transmitter request never came.
	      IER.TBE is toggled so the UART raises it.
	    - a transmitter that stays busy for three ticks: both FIFOs are
	      cleared and the modem lines are written back, the only thing left
	      to try when the shift register is stuck.

	  The guard only writes when it repairs something and reports nothing:
	  a console error cannot be announced over the console itself, and a
	  repaired console needs no announcement. Each repair is recorded with
	  LOG_DBG, which the default runtime log level keeps out of the console.

	  Note: the bring-up receive check (LS2K0300_RX_CHECK) uses internal
	  loopback on purpose and can trip this guard; keep the two apart.

config LS2K0300_IRQ_DIAG
	bool "Bring-up: dump the interrupt CSRs and time a few sleeps"
	default n
	help
	  Print CRMD/PRMD/ECFG/ESTAT over the console at APPLICATION init and then
	  sleep three times, so interrupt delivery can be checked from the console
	  alone. A bring-up aid: keep it off in production images.

endif # SOC_FAMILY_LS2K300
